To verify the correctness of the task, the contents of the /home/user/top_cited.txt file should match the expected output.

The expected output can be obtained by reading the /home/user/citation_graph/data/papers.jsonl file, building a directed graph from the data, and calculating the in-degrees of all nodes. The node with the highest in-degree will be identified as the most cited paper, and its identifier will be the expected output.

For example, if the /home/user/citation_graph/data/papers.jsonl file contains the following data:
```
{"id": "paper1", "title": "Paper 1", "references": ["paper2", "paper3"]}
{"id": "paper2", "title": "Paper 2", "references": ["paper1", "paper4"]}
{"id": "paper3", "title": "Paper 3", "references": ["paper1", "paper2"]}
{"id": "paper4", "title": "Paper 4", "references": ["paper2", "paper3"]}
```
The directed graph can be built by creating edges from each paper to its references. The in-degrees of all nodes can be calculated by counting the number of edges pointing to each node.

The node with the highest in-degree will be the most cited paper, and its identifier will be the expected output. In this example, the most cited paper is "paper1" with an in-degree of 3, so the expected output is "paper1".

The /home/user/top_cited.txt file should contain only the expected output, without any quotes or newlines.
